  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=22$ and $n=51$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(22,51)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$ To find the inverse of $22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}51 & = & 22\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 22 & = & 7\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =51-22\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =22-7\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =22-7\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22-(51-22\\cdot 2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 7+51\\cdot (-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $51,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22\\cdot 7+51\\cdot (-3) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 22\\cdot 7 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $22\\cdot 7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51),$ which means that $7$ is the multiplicative inverse of $22$ (modulo $51$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 7\\cdot 22x & ≡7\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ (7\\cdot 22)x & ≡35 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡35 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ x & ≡35 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$",
